PART I: THE SEVEN THOUSAND STEPS

The steps began where the town ended, on the far side of the bridge — gray slabs worn smooth, dented down the middle by seven thousand years of pilgrims climbing them up and down. They went up together, the whole company: Ismara, Orion, Ylva, Nora, Bjorn, Mila, GEAR, the horses, Alfonso dormant and carried. Nobody left anything behind that hadn't already been hidden — the cart locked in the stable, crystal now, and unlikely to interest anyone who found it.

The first stones were warm under their boots despite the cold, sun caught in something that wasn't quite sun. A few turns up, a stone tablet stood at a right angle in the stair — old script, readable. Before the birth of men, the dragons ruled all Mundus. Their word was the Voice, and they spoke only for true need, for the Voice could blot out the sky and flood the land.

The wrongness started small. Snow near their boots fell upward before settling back down, ordinary again a moment later. Then it wasn't small: Ismara noticed Mila had grown — an inch, more, in the time it took to blink — and pressed her Sight against her to check. No unusual aura. Nothing marking her as touched by anything Ismara could name. She turned the same look on Bjorn out of habit, and found nothing there either — not "nothing wrong," nothing at all, no trace of whatever was happening to everyone else in his direction. She asked him what he was. He didn't understand the question. He didn't know he wasn't aging with the rest of them; he had no way to know it without eyes like hers.

Ismara pressed anyway — sharper than she needed to, sharper than usual — and Bjorn had no idea why the woman wearing Davinia's face wouldn't leave him alone. He answered what he could. Told her Mila had good color from the road, working hard on that soup every night, cabbage in it more nights than not, and it seemed to be doing her good. He didn't have the first idea what she meant by aura or magic surrounding her — none of that was language that meant anything to him. Someone showed him his own reflection so he'd see it too. He looked no different to himself.

Ismara's suspicion crystallized into something the group could act on: whatever was happening ran on contact. Mila and the black mare, Balthazar, were aging at exactly the same rate — the two of them, and nothing else, moving in lockstep through it while everyone else drifted at their own pace. If touch bound people to the same rate, then binding themselves together bought protection from drifting apart. They tied the party together with rope, hand to hand, and kept climbing.

PART II: THE GUARDIAN

The line between them had already worked loose, unnoticed, by the time the path bent hard around a shoulder of rock — a narrow track with a fall on one side and open stone climbing away on the other. Four robed figures stood waiting where the bend opened, a fifth held back behind them. Ismara knew the robes on sight: the same ones worn by the Mythic Dawn cultist they'd found frozen in crystal, root-locked beneath the heart tree at the Midden. These didn't sound friendly, and didn't wait long enough to prove otherwise.

GEAR called up its ice-cased guardian to meet them and tried to read what they actually were — the horn found nothing to hold onto. They weren't solid enough to resonate at any frequency, nothing stable enough to name a strength or a weakness against. All GEAR could confirm was the plainest thing: eleven points of damage resistance, a hundred each, no tell beyond that.

Orion threw Alfonso bodily at the nearest of them, meaning to run him straight through. The cultist blinked out and caught the blade out of the air bare-handed instead — and where his skin met the rapier, something crept up his arm, hesitated, and crept back down again, refused. He dropped Alfonso rather than carry whatever that had almost been.

Ylva went in with the axe Alfonso had made her, lightning riding the swing, and put one of them down hard enough that the strike carried through to whoever stood beside him. Varon tried his own weapon's trick next — the edge that was supposed to cut straight past armor and answer only to flesh — and found nothing there to answer it. Whatever these things were built from, it wasn't meat.

The cultists fought like nothing else the company had crossed blades with before: pulling extra turns out of time itself, the same way the mountain had already been doing to the party all day, burning their own strength to buy the extra strike. It worked, and it cost them — every time one of them reached for a second action that way, it came back out of their own hide, twenty points at a stroke, spent on speed instead of armor.

It went hard on both sides. Davinia went down once and came back up on Saijah's healing; Ylva dropped hard enough to cross her own threshold and rose again mid-fight as something else entirely, claws first, driving two of them back with a single wide arc before the shape settled again. Varon pushed himself past what his body wanted to give twice over, buying the extra turns in the same coin the mountain had already been charging everyone all day. Twenty years came off him at once, nowhere else to spend them but forward. Mila, still climbing with them and still innocent of any of it, had crossed into her tenth year sometime in the middle of the exchange, and hadn't slowed down to notice.

The fight was still going when they stopped — four cultists battered but standing, the fifth untouched and waiting at the back. Nothing about it was finished yet.

PART III: WHAT THE MOUNTAIN WAS GUARDING

A round went by before anyone thought to reach for the recorder. Orion tried first — called out across the ground between them, tried to open a dialogue with the four of them before anyone had swung anything, and got nothing back for it. Calm came next, thrown at both of the ones nearest the front. It took the first one whole — something in him went out like a snuffed candle, the aggression just gone from his stance. The second one it only blunted; he was still standing, still armed, but something in him had eased off a notch, enough that he wasn't reaching for a killing blow anymore, just holding ground.

Ismara took the moment that bought and used it on herself first — Ebonyflesh drawn up over bare skin, the working settling into her like a second coat before she'd even decided whether this was a fight yet. Only then did she call out to ask what, exactly, they thought they were guarding.

They didn't answer like cultists. Four of them, and Guile turned up a fifth held back behind — Argonian, Dunmer, Nord, Altmer, standing together in a grouping that made no sense on its face until the same roll turned up why it should: the Dunmer word for Argonian is the same word Dunmer use for a farm tool, the kind of history that doesn't sit around a shared fire by accident, not among people who'd chosen each other. Ismara asked whether they'd been hired to get in someone's way. Simply a matter of duty, one of them said, and didn't elaborate. Asked if they thought of themselves as protectors of some kind: yeah, that's a good way to put it. They called it a ceremony. Private. Family, in a manner of speaking — the mountain belonged to the people, and they couldn't be interrupted right now. Asked how long, the answer came back a shrug and a joke about a foreman who turned out, on pressing, not to exist at all. One of them had worked the mines once, somewhere south. One had escaped a prison and didn't dress it up: he'd killed a lot of people to get out, and he wasn't sorry. Asked how long they'd all known each other, how long they'd actually been up on this mountain — no clean answer came back. Maybe an hour. Maybe three years. One of them, a Nord, went by Gunjar — a name handed over as easily and as carelessly as everything else they'd said.

Asked whether any of this touched the Greybeards, one of them didn't say no. He made it into an argument instead: the masters up there knew exactly what was happening on this mountain, right under their own floor, and had done nothing whatsoever to stop it. Doing nothing wasn't the same as doing nothing about it — silence had a side to it, the same as speaking did, and by that measure the order at the top of these steps was already working with them, whether the masters wanted to own that or not.

Each of them carried a shard of black crystal grown straight into their flesh — not held, not worn, grown, the way the ground itself had grown the sap climbing this whole mountain. One of them held his own shard up unprompted, like he was proud of it. Looking straight at it cost Saijah a headache that arrived all at once, both eyes, a full cluster behind the sockets before she'd even finished turning her head. Whatever the crystal was doing, it wasn't simple control. It was holding something together in each of them — keeping the worst of what the mountain does to a mind and a body from getting all the way in.

The party actually weighed walking away from it. They had a schedule. A ceremony wasn't their business, and if there was a way around, that was worth more than a fight. It didn't survive contact with what had already happened without anyone quite deciding it should: Ismara had already thrown Alfonso, by telekinesis, into the downed one's open hand, and the infection had already started climbing. It went up through the fingers, up the wrist, reaching for the crystal — and stopped there, cold, unable to get past it. Something in the shard was fighting to undo what Alfonso does to anyone he touches without even trying, a small private war being lost and won at the same time in one man's wrist while the rest of the company stood there deciding whether to fight. Guile settled what was left of the question: whatever this was, it was the same weather that had been eating Ivarstead alive the whole time the company climbed toward it. Nobody weighed walking away again after that.

The same touch that let Alfonso into that man's hand let Ismara reach through it too — the mirror-trick she'd lost two sessions back, waiting for her again, changed. Not free the way it used to be. A roll now, not a thought, something she had to actually reach for instead of simply having. She took it anyway, took the man's own hand, and made him cut his own face open with it. Before the real fighting started back up, Varon asked once, plainly, whether the crystals could just be sundered out of them instead — cut away clean the way he'd cut armor off a man without killing the man underneath it. The answer he got back closed that door for good: the crystal wasn't sitting in these people, it was woven through everything they were, blood and bone and the nerves that ran between them. Taking it out gently was the same act as taking out someone's skeleton gently. There wasn't a version of this fight that ended with these people both alive and free of it.

PART IV: THE FIGHT

It cost both sides plenty. Ylva took a blow that should have been the end of the fight for her — not a stagger, not something she'd shake off and keep swinging through, a real killing hit, the kind that puts a person on the ground and keeps them there. For one full moment she actually was that: a woman down in the snow on a mountain that had no particular interest in whether she got back up. Then Hircine's claim on her came due before anyone could reach her. She didn't rise so much as come apart and reassemble herself on her feet, her own voice going somewhere no human throat could follow it, fur coming up where skin had been a breath earlier, the armor she'd had on shredding off her in strips because none of it was ever built to survive what came up in its place. Nine feet of red-and-gold fur and fury where a person had been lying a second before, and she was already moving again before the change had even finished happening to her — one clawed swing enough to put a man flat on his back.

Varon put a second one over the edge of the mountain outright — braced against the rock, unhurried in the way of someone who has done this exact motion enough times that his body knows it better than his mind does, though it never once stopped costing him anything to do it. The company's Ice Atronach, summoned and directed through the fight by GEAR, picked a third clean off his feet and carried him the rest of the way to the drop, over a grab for rescue that came a half-second too late to matter.

The one who hadn't lost anyone yet — the Shard Zealot, the only one of the five who'd shown real power the whole fight — watched two of his own go over that edge and called out to what was left of them in a single word: double time. He wasn't casting anything when he said it. It was coordination, nothing more, spoken aloud only so the others would know what pace he'd chosen for all of them. They matched him, and it cost every one of them plainly — burned down toward nothing to buy the extra motion, the same coin the mountain had been charging the party all day, just spent on purpose instead of endured. One locked Ylva inside a shell of ash she couldn't break out of. One reached for Orion with telekinesis and tried to pitch him off the mountain the same way Varon had just pitched one of theirs — Orion felt the pull take hold before his own weight answered it back, and held, boots skidding right up to the edge of the drop before it let go of him.

Alfonso, briefly given a body of his own inside the Atronach, fought from inside it a while — until the Zealot tore him back out with telekinesis, wrenched him loose entirely and left him hanging in the air, and tried to drive him into Varon like a thrown blade. It should have worked. It didn't: the Zealot was still supposed to be calm, and turning Alfonso into a weapon broke that the same way swinging a fist would have. The throw went wide. Alfonso ended up pinned to the rock wall near Varon instead of buried in him, and stayed there, held, until Orion's Song of Dissonance broke the Zealot's concentration outright and let him drop free.

The Zealot dual-cast a firestorm wide enough to have caught half the company at once. Ismara caught it instead — a reaction nobody at that fire had seen her use before, distortion thrown into the air around the blast, and what came through on the other side of it landed at half of what it should have. Nora took what was left of it anyway and went down hard, a frame never built to absorb something that size even halved. The Atronach, built of nothing fire could touch, walked out of it without a mark.

The Zealot grew a blade straight out of his own crystal growths, the same substance climbing out of his own arm, and used it on Ismara three times running before she got clear of him. The Ebonyflesh she'd raised before any of this started was the only thing standing between her and something far worse — and even with it up, the second strike put her down onto one knee, both hands braced flat against the stone, before the wardings closed around what was left of the blow and she managed to get back up.

The Zealot reached for one more dual-cast, wide enough this time to catch his own remaining people along with the party — and it did. One of his own died from it outright, cut down by the same spell meant to finish the fight, before the company had laid a hand on him. Ismara answered what was left of that man with something she'd been holding since the second dragon they ever fought — a sealed canister of dragonfire, kept back since the Midden — and let all of it go on him while he was already dying. It didn't just burn him. It bled him at the same time, the two things happening together, neither one waiting for the other to finish.

The one still pinned to the Atronach, Alfonso's blade still lodged clean through his hand, kept casting fire into himself with nowhere else left to aim it, over and over, until a wild roll took the hand clean off and the fire that followed took the rest of him with it. The Zealot healed himself once, bought a little more time with it, reached for Alfonso a second time and lost him again to the same song, went down paralyzed under Ismara's hand, and died under Saijah's bow — one arrow, drawn with Bjorn's strength behind it as much as her own, enough force behind it to carry him bodily off the mountain still burning as he went.

When it was finally over, the ones the shards had marked didn't lie there the way bodies do. Cut open for the crystal woven through them, the flesh let go of whatever the shard had been holding it to all at once — aged forward and backward and forward again in the space of one breath, decades of it compressed into seconds, until there was simply nothing left to bury.

Nobody noticed for a long while that GEAR himself had gone quiet somewhere in the middle of all of it. Unable to climb — he has no legs to climb with — he'd gone over the edge at some point trying to hold his own ground, caught himself the only way he could by clinging to whatever pulls him toward the buried metal running under this whole stretch of Skyrim, and by the time anyone thought to actually look for him, he'd hung there, fallen the rest of the way, and started climbing back up alone — all of it, unnoticed, for the better part of twenty minutes while the rest of the company knelt over a corpse a few feet from the edge and didn't think to check.

PART V: WHAT THEY TOOK FROM IT

They gave what they'd cut free of the body to Alfonso — two hundred and six bones, laced through with the same crystal that had held the man together against his own borrowed time, and found that Alfonso could stand inside them the way he'd once stood inside GEAR. A second body. This one built from the very thing that had spent the whole fight trying to keep the mountain safe from him.

They went through the camp after, the same way they'd already gone through it once from a distance: the cold fire-scar, the worn bedding, every position on that ground still watching downhill, out toward the town, the way it had been watching since before the party ever reached the bottom of the Steps. A villager's body turned up separately, a sap-shard driven into the sternum the same way the guards' had been, and it came apart the instant the shard came free of it. Pulling that one loose cost Saijah something too — her migraine sharpened the moment the crystal stopped being housed in a body at all, one more point taken off whichever part of her she could still spare it from. Coin. Rations. Two pints of hunting mead. A coil of good rope and a bag of pitons, better than what they already had. A note, folded in among the rest of it, that confirmed plainly what the company had already worked out for themselves the hard way, without anyone writing it down for them first: tied together, whatever this mountain did to one of them, it did to all of them the same, one shared result instead of four separate ones each carried alone. And two blades, neither steel nor glass but somehow both at once, sharper than either should have been able to make sense of on its own, nothing any smith or enchanter in Skyrim would know what to do with.

Orion had found them both and given one to Varon without making anything of it — here, take this. Varon carried it exactly as far as Saijah and stopped there, held it out, and didn't say anything else. His hand wasn't quite steady on a weapon he'd fought an entire battle without shaking once. She didn't say anything back either — looked at him, then at the blade, and took it with a nod and nothing more than that. He turned away first, found Ismara across the camp, and lifted a thumbs-up at her like a man reporting back after finishing an assignment he'd needed real coaching to get through — because he had. She'd told him once, plainly, to get Saijah something, to be kind to her. He'd finally managed it.

Ylva, still wearing her other shape, told the one who'd earned it that they'd fought well and that the enemy had cheated the whole way through — the same fury from the ambush lower on the mountain, back whole and undiminished. Orion said what needed saying to the company's own — not to Varon, who has never once waited to be told before acting — but to Nora, to Bjorn, to whichever of them might be standing closest the next time one of their own went over an edge: from here on, nobody needed permission to help. If someone on this company was in danger, that was reason enough on its own.

Mila had aged again, into her twelfth year, a body that had gone through everything a body goes through at twelve in the space of one climb, while whatever was actually running things behind her eyes stayed exactly where it had always been. Nora looked closer to thirty than she had that morning, still shaking from how close the fight had come to finishing her outright. Bjorn had not aged with the rest of them — and it wasn't the first time that had come up between him and Ismara either. She'd asked him about it once already on this same climb, and asked again now, the same question put to him twice in one day, and got nothing back either time that explained anything. He didn't have an answer. He never has. Varon looked no older than he had that morning, elf that he is, but the surgery under his stolen face was starting to show its wear regardless, skin that was never meant to be worn this long finally starting to look it. The horses had aged with everyone else — Balthazar heavier through the shoulder than he'd been at the bottom of the Steps, Mable grey now and favoring a leg she hadn't favored before.

They stopped where the guards had, because that was as far as the night had gotten them — not to rest. Nothing on this mountain was going to let them do that.
